WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.060
Scientists purified a protein that, with EGF, made normal cells grow like tumour

2
00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.540
cells. They named it transforming growth factor beta.

3
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.920
Then the same protein was caught holding other cells back.

4
00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.520
What does TGF-β beta do to a cell once it lands?

5
00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.380
Follow its message from the membrane to a gene.

6
00:00:23.183 --> 00:00:26.983
The receptors for TGF-β -beta are kinases of two types.

7
00:00:27.443 --> 00:00:30.923
The type 2 kinase is active before any ligand arrives.

8
00:00:31.703 --> 00:00:34.983
TGF-β -beta is a dimer, two chains joined together.

9
00:00:35.443 --> 00:00:37.283
It binds two type 2 receptors.

10
00:00:38.023 --> 00:00:41.903
Then the ligand and type 2 together recruit two type 1 receptors.

11
00:00:42.503 --> 00:00:45.023
Four receptor chains now surround one ligand.

12
00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:51.207
Type 2 adds phosphate to a stretch of type 1 just inside the membrane, called the GS

13
00:00:51.207 --> 00:00:56.627
domain. At rest, the GS domain holds the type 1 kinase in an inactive shape.

14
00:00:57.347 --> 00:00:58.707
Phosphate ends that arrangement.

15
00:00:59.347 --> 00:01:03.167
The kinase turns on, and the stretch becomes a docking site for a Smad.

16
00:01:03.627 --> 00:01:06.447
Think of type 2 as a lit match, and type 1 as a fuse.

17
00:01:06.907 --> 00:01:09.067
The ligand only holds the match to the fuse.

18
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.880
The active type 1 kinase phosphorylates a Smad.

19
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:18.140
A Smad is itself a transcription factor, a protein that binds DNA.

20
00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:25.460
TGF-β beta receptors phosphorylate Smad2 and Smad3, on two serines at the end of the

21
00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:29.380
tail. A phosphorylated tail fits a pocket on a neighbouring Smad.

22
00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:34.260
So the Smads leave the receptor, and typically two of them join one Smad4.

23
00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:38.453
The complex collects in the nucleus and binds DNA.

24
00:01:39.033 --> 00:01:43.133
But it reads a sequence just a few base pairs long, and holds it weakly.

25
00:01:43.533 --> 00:01:48.873
That sequence turns up millions of times in a human genome, so a Smad complex alone

26
00:01:48.873 --> 00:01:50.093
cannot pick a gene.

27
00:01:50.613 --> 00:01:55.473
It binds firmly mostly next to partner proteins, and each cell type makes its own

28
00:01:55.473 --> 00:02:02.433
partners. In many epithelial cells, the partners turn on p15 and p21, and the cell

29
00:02:02.433 --> 00:02:07.533
stops dividing. In a fibroblast, the same pathway turns on collagen genes.

30
00:02:08.417 --> 00:02:10.597
The ligand brings two kinases together.

31
00:02:11.317 --> 00:02:15.937
Type 2 phosphorylates type 1 and type 1 phosphorylates a Smad.

32
00:02:16.537 --> 00:02:17.977
The steps run one way.

33
00:02:18.737 --> 00:02:22.737
In each cell, the partners decide what the message of the Smad means.

34
00:02:23.417 --> 00:02:27.177
Keep this rule: the ligand only introduces two kinases.
